1
00:00:00,172 --> 00:00:07,227
Surely if there's ever a character with which one can have colorful outrageous fun on a red carpet, it's Ellsworth's.

2
00:00:07,227 --> 00:00:09,247
Imagine all these dresses are dinner guests.

3
00:00:09,247 --> 00:00:11,411
Who's the one who's going to be the most fun?

4
00:00:11,411 --> 00:00:13,133
It's good to think of clothes like that.

5
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:13,946
Hello everyone.

6
00:00:13,946 --> 00:00:23,676
I'm Rosman Pike and I'm here with Harper's Bizarre UK and I'm about to take a little look back on some of the characters, some of the roles that have left a lasting impact on me.

7
00:00:23,676 --> 00:00:29,295
Here goes.

8
00:00:29,295 --> 00:00:37,025
Gone Girl, which came out in 2014, was a huge turning point in in my life.&nbsp; 《

9
00:00:37,025 --> 00:00:41,521
I I I realize now that that there must have been so many people who wanted that role.

10
00:00:41,521 --> 00:00:45,517
I couldn't say that I was better than anybody else or I did a better audition.

11
00:00:45,517 --> 00:00:51,452
I just think that David Fincher homes in on what he wants and it happened to be me for reasons that still mystify me.

12
00:00:51,452 --> 00:01:00,271
I think if if if I'd been too big an actress at that point, people might not have believed that um I would have signed on to play the dead girl.

13
00:01:00,271 --> 00:01:04,977
But I think at that point in my career, everyone thought Rosmund Pike would definitely sign on to play the dead girl.

14
00:01:04,977 --> 00:01:07,768
It was a very strange casting process for it.

15
00:01:07,768 --> 00:01:09,149
Um very secretive.

16
00:01:09,149 --> 00:01:15,886
David Fincher operates in mysterious ways and it involved me flying secretly whilst on another job to St. Louis.&nbsp; David Fincher

17
00:01:15,886 --> 00:01:22,239
Unbeknownst to my agents, my managers, the people I was working with at the time, it was a total secret operation.

18
00:01:22,239 --> 00:01:27,976
And on this flight over there, I got this email and the title of the email was for your eyes only.

19
00:01:27,976 --> 00:01:30,620
And there was the first script of this film.

20
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:37,179
And I mean, it was it was a sort of real pinch me moment and very exciting and nerve-wracking and I was totally out of my depth.

21
00:01:37,179 --> 00:01:44,598
It was such a fun thing to do as well as being nerve-wracking because she's so histrionic and she's so she's so performative,&nbsp;

22
00:01:44,598 --> 00:01:55,579
you know, she performs vulnerability, she performs sexiness, she performs the kind of media darling, she gives you every part of being a woman in one character.

23
00:01:55,579 --> 00:02:01,546
It's just it became kind of giddy to play because she's such a performer and her power is so great.

24
00:02:01,546 --> 00:02:05,174
Her husband's scared of her and I think I was scared of her to be honest.

25
00:02:05,174 --> 00:02:08,678
It's still the character I'm most grateful for.

26
00:02:08,678 --> 00:02:14,800
I think it's probably one of those ones that if I watched it back now, which of I have not and I haven't seen it for many years,&nbsp;

27
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:21,750
it would probably be one of those things that I look back and think I'll be much kinder to myself in retrospect than I was at the time. ——

28
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:28,823
You know, at the time I just saw all the problems to my mind, but I'm sure if I look back now, I I might think that I did okay.

29
00:02:28,823 --> 00:02:33,321
Saltburn was a total phenomenon in in more ways than one.&nbsp; 《Saltburn》

30
00:02:33,321 --> 00:02:35,698
For a start, it was a magical thing to film.

31
00:02:35,698 --> 00:02:45,672
We had a very magical summer filming in one house in Northamptonshire with a cast who became instantly very close.

32
00:02:45,672 --> 00:02:49,500
But then there was this whole thing of of it being consumed on social media.

33
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:52,812
And I think many many people who tell me they've seen Thorburn have not seen Thorburn.

34
00:02:52,812 --> 00:02:55,515
I think a lot of people have just consumed clips.

35
00:02:55,515 --> 00:03:02,229
You know, Ellsworth has, you know, become iconic for her humor and her shallowess and her vanity.

36
00:03:02,229 --> 00:03:07,550
But she's also a woman who underwent an unspeakable tragedy.

37
00:03:07,550 --> 00:03:20,270
So, you know, all the while while filming this thing that's become something that's now the subject of parties and people doing themed Saltburn evenings and dressing up as Ellsworth and lip syncing my lines on Tik Tok.

38
00:03:20,270 --> 00:03:27,067
While filming it, I was also up late at night in the chat rooms of women who've lost children.

39
00:03:27,067 --> 00:03:37,823
the people who have generously shared on social media about how you can continue to live when you've suffered the loss of a of of a teenage or adult child.

40
00:03:37,823 --> 00:03:45,447
Um the internet has become an amazing resource for actors who want to understand deeply an authentic experience.

41
00:03:45,447 --> 00:03:50,710
So for me that was another reality of filming Sburn. That's a reason why Elizabeth lingers with me.

42
00:03:50,710 --> 00:03:56,489
That that is will be surprising I think to some people maybe the people who didn't see the whole film.

43
00:03:56,489 --> 00:04:07,646
The press tour for Saltburn was a was a joy because my friend and collaborator Leath Clark said to me, "We've got to, you know, channel Ellsworth with this tour.&nbsp;

44
00:04:07,646 --> 00:04:11,783
We've got to think, you know, what dresses do we want to take to the party?" "

45
00:04:11,783 --> 00:04:18,913
Surely if there's ever a character with which one can have colorful, outrageous fun on a red carpet, it's Ellsworth.

46
00:04:18,913 --> 00:04:34,590
So that involved blue sequined Rodarte at the Critics' Choice Awards, to a bold silk green pleated dress with a sequin gold cape for a sort of just a normal dinner.

47
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:38,453
Because you know who doesn't want to wear a sequin gold cape to dinner?

48
00:04:38,453 --> 00:04:48,602
Almost at a funeral at the Golden Globes with the veil and the décolletage was actually to solve a problem which was that I'd had a skiing accident and smashed up my face.

49
00:04:48,602 --> 00:04:59,112
So you know I had a huge wound on my chin which Philip Treacy's veil did a very good job at concealing.

50
00:04:59,112 --> 00:05:02,292
The spirit was imagine all these dresses are dinner guests.

51
00:05:02,292 --> 00:05:05,106
You know who's the one who's going to be the most fun?

52
00:05:05,106 --> 00:05:07,397
It's good to think of clothes like that.

53
00:05:07,397 --> 00:05:11,755
[Jane Austin] With Jane Austen, I genuinely believe that there is magic in her stories.

54
00:05:11,755 --> 00:05:20,918
I genuinely believe that everybody who participates in whatever adaptation of a Jane Austen it is has a kind of enchanted time.

55
00:05:20,918 --> 00:05:32,393
I think whenever you have a chance to do an adaptation of a book, it's a gift for an actor because there is such a wealth of information at your fingertips and there's a whole head of an author to dive inside.

56
00:05:32,393 --> 00:05:40,356
We were filming at this beautiful house called Groombridge which was near Tunbridge Wells and there was a meadow right behind the house.

57
00:05:40,356 --> 00:05:46,726
There was a moat around the house and there was a green that you could roam over to a lovely pub in the village.

58
00:05:46,726 --> 00:05:56,928
And it was one of those golden summers and we were all very young and it was most people's sort of one of their first jobs if not their very first job.

59
00:05:56,928 --> 00:06:05,661
We had Donald Sutherland as our father who did magical things like remember he had a Rolls-Royce Silver Shadow in storage in somewhere in the north of England.

60
00:06:05,661 --> 00:06:10,692
And that sort of masterpiece of engineering came out and was driven down to Tunbridge Wells.

61
00:06:10,692 --> 00:06:18,301
Jane and Lizzie shared a bedroom and Kier and I would escape to this bedroom upstairs and chat while other scenes were going on.&nbsp;

62
00:06:18,301 --> 00:06:24,834
I think Jane Bennet is quite a magical character although she's the less vibrant one than Lizzy.

63
00:06:24,834 --> 00:06:28,033
I think it imbues a sense of calm.

64
00:06:28,033 --> 00:06:35,717
I'm not a particularly calm person, but Jane sort of can instill a sense of calm because she chooses to see the best in people.

65
00:06:35,717 --> 00:06:37,546
And I think it's a very lovely quality.

66
00:06:37,546 --> 00:06:48,768
So, I found Jane a very good companion for a summer.

67
00:06:48,768 --> 00:06:55,820
[Massive Attack] I was lucky enough to be asked to do this very unusual piece of work which was a music video for Massive Attack.

68
00:06:55,820 --> 00:06:59,511
They were releasing a new EP with a band called the Young Fathers.

69
00:06:59,511 --> 00:07:02,909
And they sent me this track called Voodoo in My Blood.

70
00:07:02,909 --> 00:07:15,933
And Ringan Ledwidge, who was a brilliant commercials director, totally inspired genius of a man who's sadly no longer with us, said, "I want to do something in the subway at Euston station..."&nbsp;

71
00:07:15,933 --> 00:07:21,354
I want it to be a meeting with some strange force in the subway.

72
00:07:21,354 --> 00:07:27,717
He said, "I think it's going to be a big silver ball, but it's got to be about addiction and our addiction to technology."

73
00:07:27,717 --> 00:07:35,466
I just finished a film called A United Kingdom, and we had to learn to lindy hop and do those 1940s dances.

74
00:07:35,466 --> 00:07:45,178
There was a dance choreographer called Scarlett Mackman who taught me to lindy hop and got me kind of in my body in a way that nobody else has really ever.

75
00:07:45,178 --> 00:07:46,981
He somehow released something.

76
00:07:46,981 --> 00:07:53,756
And I thought, if there's one person who can channel the voodoo with me for this Massive Attack video, it's this person.

77
00:07:53,756 --> 00:07:56,506
So I rang her up, not knowing her very well at all.

78
00:07:56,506 --> 00:08:03,194
And I said, I don't know, I've got this hunch that you might have an inkling about, you know, how to communicate a bit of voodoo.

79
00:08:03,194 --> 00:08:13,338
And she said, "Oh my," she said, "You've come to the right person."

80
00:08:13,338 --> 00:08:20,498
And we had a day basically to, or maybe not even a day, maybe a few hours to kind of create something and then the next day we were going to film it.

81
00:08:20,498 --> 00:08:28,751
So, it was one of those times where a short space of time and a time pressure can yield something truly creative.

82
00:08:28,751 --> 00:08:34,640
And where you sort of push the boundaries of yourself and what you feel you're capable of and who you are.

83
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:39,005
And you just have to throw yourself in and commit 100% wholeheartedly.

84
00:08:39,005 --> 00:08:44,329
The next day we were in Euston underpass with the track blaring.

85
00:08:44,329 --> 00:08:48,070
Because Ringan understood that you need that track blaring through Euston underpass.

86
00:08:48,070 --> 00:08:53,028
We created this mad thing that I'm really proud of.

87
00:08:53,028 --> 00:08:58,972
I think because it's one of the things that I look back and I have no idea where it came from and I have no idea whether I could even do it again.

88
00:08:58,972 --> 00:09:03,137
And those are the most exciting pieces of work where you literally don't know where it's come from.

89
00:09:03,137 --> 00:09:08,029
You've just been a vessel and something has come through you and you know it's you.

90
00:09:08,029 --> 00:09:10,081
You know you've done it but you have no idea.

91
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:12,617
You felt like you were no part of it.

92
00:09:12,617 --> 00:09:14,635
Almost like an out-of-body experience.

93
00:09:14,635 --> 00:09:23,897
In 2018 I played Mary Colvin who was a brilliant fiercely independent war correspondent for the Sunday Times.&nbsp;

94
00:09:23,897 --> 00:09:36,372
She did long form investigative journalism which meant that she would really embed with communities particularly civilians affected by conflict and war.

95
00:09:36,372 --> 00:09:42,849
And she would give voice to people who feared that their perspective would never be out in the wider world.

96
00:09:42,849 --> 00:09:45,456
You know she also gained the trust of world leaders.

97
00:09:45,456 --> 00:09:51,352
You know she repeatedly had a set of pearls given to her by Yasser Arafat but she was a very interesting person to play.

98
00:09:51,352 --> 00:09:57,442
She lost an eye in Sri Lanka when she was going into Tamil territory in the north.

99
00:09:57,442 --> 00:10:04,463
And so she wore an eye patch, which is how some people who don't know her name still identify her and say, "Oh, the journalist with the eye patch."

100
00:10:04,463 --> 00:10:10,774
But obviously playing her meant that I had to create a sort of physical embodiment of this woman.

101
00:10:10,774 --> 00:10:12,611
It couldn't be a performance.

102
00:10:12,611 --> 00:10:15,640
It had to be a becoming of someone else.

103
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:23,961
Which I guess acting always is to some degree, but in this respect, there was a prototype or the real thing that you could cross refer to.

104
00:10:23,961 --> 00:10:25,998
You have to feel that you are them.

105
00:10:25,998 --> 00:10:27,972
So, they become more real to you than you do.

106
00:10:27,972 --> 00:10:30,672
Another thing for an actress is Marie was older than me.

107
00:10:30,672 --> 00:10:40,098
So, a lot of an actress's life is spent trying to look younger, trying to keep one's looks, all those things.

108
00:10:40,098 --> 00:10:44,074
And well, so people think in fact it's not about that at all.

109
00:10:44,074 --> 00:10:47,100
There is inevitably focus on appearance.

110
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:50,240
There's inevitably focus on holding on to your appearance.

111
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:54,143
But actually where freedom lies is in a totally other direction.

112
00:10:54,143 --> 00:11:06,410
So playing Marie where I actually was too young for the part and had to make myself older then unleashed this whole period of my life where I played a few people who were older than me.

113
00:11:06,410 --> 00:11:08,171
I played Mary Curie as well.

114
00:11:08,171 --> 00:11:10,666
I actually found it wonderfully liberating.

115
00:11:10,666 --> 00:11:14,073
And I thought, well, here you go. You can cheat time in multiple directions.

116
00:11:14,073 --> 00:11:19,392
It doesn't always have to be about cheating time and pretending that we have to go back and hold on to our youth.

117
00:11:19,392 --> 00:11:25,164
We can actually cheat time and get to somewhere that we're going too soon and then we get another go at it.

118
00:11:25,164 --> 00:11:28,964
You know, we can play it and then we can live it.

119
00:11:28,964 --> 00:11:34,879
So, I think that was a big teaching, a big lesson that playing Marie taught me.

120
00:11:34,879 --> 00:11:41,412
Thank you so much for coming with me on this little exploratory journey through some important roles.

121
00:11:41,412 --> 00:11:43,912
I hope by the time you're seeing this I will have been heavily edited so I haven't wasted too much of your time.

